WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.951
Node.

2
00:00:00.951 --> 00:00:10.377
js is a common platform for APIs, web applications, microservices, automation, serverless functions, and backend-for-frontend systems.

3
00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:16.952
It often sits close to users, data, identity, payments, internal services, and third-party APIs.

4
00:00:16.952 --> 00:00:18.061
That makes Node.

5
00:00:18.061 --> 00:00:26.537
js security a practical concern for developers and operators, not a specialty topic that can be pushed to the end of the project.

6
00:00:26.537 --> 00:00:30.814
Running JavaScript on the server changes the security picture.

7
00:00:30.814 --> 00:00:41.587
The application may parse untrusted requests, read environment variables, call databases, process files, launch background work, and talk to other services.

8
00:00:41.587 --> 00:00:46.102
Asynchronous execution and event-driven architecture help Node.

9
00:00:46.102 --> 00:00:56.955
js handle many concurrent operations, but they also require careful thinking about errors, shared state, timeouts, resource limits, and operational behavior.

10
00:00:56.955 --> 00:00:57.905
Node.

11
00:00:57.905 --> 00:01:00.836
js development is package-heavy by design.

12
00:01:00.836 --> 00:01:10.263
Teams can move quickly because npm gives access to a large ecosystem, but every dependency becomes part of the application's trust boundary.

13
00:01:10.263 --> 00:01:18.184
Application security, runtime security, dependency security, and deployment security all meet in the same service.

14
00:01:18.184 --> 00:01:27.927
A safe route handler can still be harmed by weak secrets management, vulnerable middleware, unsafe package scripts, or noisy production errors.

15
00:01:27.927 --> 00:01:35.294
The goal is reliable software that handles untrusted input, secrets, users, files, and dependencies safely.

16
00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:39.413
That does not mean memorizing every possible vulnerability.

17
00:01:39.413 --> 00:01:56.920
It means designing explicit boundaries, validating runtime data, enforcing authorization on the server, managing dependencies deliberately, protecting secrets, and making production behavior observable without turning the application into a data leak.
